= Some questions on circular motion - centripetal force including vertical circles

+ 
  + \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \
    A cyclist leans over while cornering to shift their center of mass towards the
    center point of the corner. As the distance to the center of the corner
    decreases, the force needed to successfully complete the corner at some speed
    decreases proportional to the distance to the center.

  + The centripetal force $F_c$ can be found by
    $
      F_c &= (m v^2)/r \
          &= (80 dot 21^2)/(32) \
          &= 1100 "N" \
    $

  + Towards the center point of the corner.

  + $R cos theta = 785 "N"$, and $R sin theta = 1100 "N"$

    $therefore tan theta = 1100/785$; so $theta = 55 degree$.

    So $R = 785 / cos(55) = 1350 "N"$.
  + Friction


+ 
  + $ 
      F_c  &= (m v^2)/r \
      0.9m g &= (m v^2)/r \
      r &= (31^2)/(0.9 dot 9.81) \
        &= 109 "N"
    $

  + The car may roll or the back wheels may skid out, causing the driver to lose
    control.

  + The F1 cars provide downforce, which increases the vertical force on the tires.
    This causes the maximum frictional force that the tires can exert on the ground
    to be much higher, so the cars can move faster around tighter corners.

+ 
  + $omega = (2 pi)/(30 dot 60) = 3.49 dot 10^(-3) " rad s"^(-1)$

  + Assuming that $135$m is the height of the wheel,
    $
      F_c &= (65 dot (3.49 dot 10^(-3))^2 dot 67.5) \
          &= 0.05 "N"
    $

  + At the highest point: gravity.  
    
    At the lowest point: the reaction force of the car on the person.

    Halfway: the frictional force of the person's shoes against the floor of the car.

  + At the top, since at the bottom the reaction force = $m g + F_c$, where $F_c$ is
    the centrifugal force and $m g$ is the weight.


+ 
  + We know that $F_c >= m g$.
    
    $
      (m v^2)/r &>= m g \
      v^2/r &>= g \
      v &>= sqrt(g r) \
        &>= sqrt(g dot 3.4) \
        &>= 5.78 "ms"^(-1) 
    $

    So $min(v) = 5.78 "ms"^(-1)$

